<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C10.S1 — The complexity landscape</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C10.S1 — Counting and classifying</p></div><p>(a) A general \(n\)-qubit Hamiltonian is expanded in the Pauli basis. Give the number of terms, in terms of <code>n</code>.</p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Evaluate that for \(n={@n@}\).</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) A BQP algorithm has success probability \(2/3\). By the Chernoff bound, the number of repetitions needed for failure probability \(\delta\) scales as \(c\cdot\log(1/\delta)\). Give the exponent \(\alpha\) in "repetitions \(=\Theta(\log^{\alpha}(1/\delta))\)".</p><p>\(\alpha=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The Pauli strings \(\sigma_{\mu_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\sigma_{\mu_n}\) with \(\mu_i\in\{I,X,Y,Z\}\) number \(4^{n}\), and they form an orthogonal basis of the \(4^{n}\)-dimensional real space of Hermitian operators.</p><p>(b) For \(n={@n@}\): \({@ta2@}\).</p><p>(c) \(\alpha=1\): majority voting over \(O(\log(1/\delta))\) runs suffices, so the constant \(2/3\) in the definition of BQP is arbitrary. Physically relevant Hamiltonians are \(k\)-local with only \(O(n^{k})\) terms — the exponential count above is why <em>arbitrary</em> Hamiltonians are hopeless and structure is everything.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
