  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C10.S2 — The parameter-shift rule</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C10.S2 — Exact gradients from shifted circuits</p></div><p>Let \(U(\theta)=e^{-i\theta P/2}\) with \(P^{2}=I\), and let \(f(\theta)=\langle 0|U^{\dagger}(\theta)\,A\,U(\theta)|0\rangle\).</p><p>(a) Write \(U(\theta)\) in the form \(\alpha(\theta)I-i\beta(\theta)P\) and give \(\alpha(\theta)\).</p><p>\(\alpha(\theta)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) The parameter-shift rule reads \(f\'(\theta)=r\,[f(\theta+s)-f(\theta-s)]\). Give the ordered pair <code>[r, s]</code>.</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) \(f\) is a trigonometric polynomial in \(\theta\). Give its period.</p><p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Since \(P^2=I\), splitting the exponential series gives \(U(\theta)=\cos(\theta/2)I-i\sin(\theta/2)P\), so \(\alpha=\cos(\theta/2)\) — the same identity as in Chapter 1.</p><p>(b) Expanding, \(f(\theta)=a+b\cos\theta+c\sin\theta\). Then \(f(\theta+\pi/2)-f(\theta-\pi/2)=-2b\sin\theta+2c\cos\theta=2f\'(\theta)\), so \(r=1/2\) and \(s=\pi/2\).</p><p>(c) \(2\pi\), from the same expansion.</p><p>Note the rule is <em>exact</em>, not a finite-difference approximation: it evaluates the same circuit at two shifted parameter values, so it adds no depth and introduces no discretisation bias — only shot noise.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
