  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C10.S3 — Shot budgets and barren plateaus</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C10.S3 — Why NISQ is hard, quantitatively</p></div><p>(a) Estimating \(\langle A\rangle\) with \(A^{2}=I\) to additive error \(\varepsilon\) requires how many shots, at leading order in <code>eps</code>? (Use variance \(\le 1\).)</p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) A chemistry Hamiltonian has \(M\) Pauli terms measured independently, each to error \(\varepsilon/\sqrt M\). Give the total shot count in terms of <code>M</code> and <code>eps</code>.</p><p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) In a barren plateau the gradient variance scales as \(2^{-n}\). Give the number of shots needed to resolve the gradient sign, in terms of <code>n</code> (leading order).</p><p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The sample mean of a bounded random variable has standard error \(\sigma/\sqrt N\), so \(N=\Theta(1/\varepsilon^{2})\).</p><p>(b) Each of the \(M\) terms needs \(M/\varepsilon^{2}\) shots, for a total of \(M^{2}/\varepsilon^{2}\). With \(M=O(n^{4})\) spin-orbital terms and chemical accuracy \(\varepsilon=1.6\times10^{-3}\) Ha, this reaches \(10^{9}\)–\(10^{12}\) shots for molecules of interest — the arithmetic behind most scepticism about near-term VQE. Grouping commuting terms into cliques reduces \(M\), but not the asymptotic form.</p><p>(c) Resolving a quantity of size \(2^{-n/2}\) needs \(N\sim2^{n}\) shots: gradient estimation becomes exponentially expensive, which is exactly the barren-plateau obstruction. Note that this is a statement about <em>trainability</em>, independent of whether the ansatz could in principle represent the ground state.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
